(window.DETAIL_C = window.DETAIL_C || {})["phy-11-08"] = {"phy-11-08-m01":{"idea":"Light travels extremely fast. In vacuum its speed is a fixed number, c.","steps":["The speed of light in vacuum is c = 3 × 10⁸ m/s.","That is 300 million metres every second.","In glass or water light is slower."],"others":"340 m/s is the speed of sound in air. 3 × 10⁶ and 3 × 10¹⁰ m/s are wrong powers of ten.","trap":"Remember the power: 10⁸, not 10⁶ or 10¹⁰.","answer":"3 × 10⁸ m/s"},"phy-11-08-m02":{"idea":"The laws of reflection have two parts: equal angles, and all three lines in one plane.","steps":["Angle of incidence equals angle of reflection, both measured from the normal.","The incident ray, the reflected ray and the normal lie in the same plane.","That second part is the one the question asks for."],"others":"Twice the angle is wrong; the angles are equal. Different planes is wrong. A reflected ray along the normal would only happen for a ray hitting at 0°.","trap":"Angles are always measured from the normal, not from the surface.","answer":"the incident ray, the reflected ray and the normal lie in the same plane"},"phy-11-08-m03":{"idea":"Two inclined plane mirrors form several images, and the count depends on the angle between them.","steps":["The number of images is n = 360°/θ − 1 when 360/θ is an even whole number.","Here θ = 60°, so 360/60 = 6.","n = 6 − 1 = 5."],"others":"4, 6 and 3 come from using a wrong angle or forgetting to subtract 1.","trap":"Do not forget the minus 1: the 6 positions include the object itself.","answer":"5"},"phy-11-08-m04":{"idea":"A plane mirror always gives the same kind of image: virtual, upright, and as far behind as the object is in front.","steps":["The reflected rays only seem to come from behind the mirror, so the image is virtual.","It is upright and the same size as the object.","It lies as far behind the mirror as the object is in front."],"others":"A plane mirror image is not magnified. It is also not real, because it cannot be shown on a screen. It is not inverted.","trap":"The image is laterally inverted: left and right are swapped.","answer":"virtual, erect, and as far behind the mirror as the object is in front"},"phy-11-08-m05":{"idea":"A spherical mirror focuses at half its radius of curvature.","steps":["A ray aimed at the centre of curvature returns along itself.","The focus is half-way between the pole and the centre of curvature.","So f = R/2."],"others":"2R, R/4 and R do not match the half-way geometry.","trap":"f = R/2 holds for rays close to the axis.","answer":"f = R/2"},"phy-11-08-m06":{"idea":"The mirror formula links the focal length with the object and image distances.","steps":["For a spherical mirror: 1/f = 1/v + 1/u.","Use the same sign convention for all three distances.","The magnification is m = −v/u."],"others":"f = v + u is not dimensionally the same relation. 1/v − 1/u² mixes powers. 1/f = v/u is also wrong.","trap":"The mirror formula has a plus sign. The lens formula has a minus sign.","answer":"1/f = 1/v + 1/u, with signs from the sign convention"},"phy-11-08-m07":{"idea":"An object at the centre of curvature gives an image at the centre of curvature, the same size, inverted.","steps":["The centre of curvature is at u = 2f.","1/v = 1/f − 1/(2f) = 1/(2f), so v = 2f.","The magnification is −1: same size, inverted, real."],"others":"A magnified image beyond C is for an object between F and C. A real erect image at the focus does not occur here. Virtual and erect is what a mirror forms for an object inside the focus.","trap":"At C: image at C, same size, inverted.","answer":"real, inverted, of the same size, and at the centre of curvature"},"phy-11-08-m08":{"idea":"Refractive index compares the speed of light in vacuum with its speed in the medium.","steps":["Definition: n = c/v.","In a material, v is less than c, so n is greater than 1.","A larger n means slower light."],"others":"v/c is the inverse and would give a number less than 1. Product and difference of speeds have no meaning here.","trap":"n is always at least 1 for a material.","answer":"c/v, the speed of light in vacuum to the speed in the medium"},"phy-11-08-m09":{"idea":"Snell's law connects the angles in two media through their refractive indices.","steps":["Measure both angles from the normal.","The law is n₁ sin i = n₂ sin r.","Going into a denser medium (bigger n), the ray bends towards the normal."],"others":"n₁ sin r = n₂ sin i swaps the angles. The forms with 1/sin and cos are not Snell's law.","trap":"The index and its own angle go together on each side.","answer":"n₁ sin i = n₂ sin r"},"phy-11-08-m10":{"idea":"A pond looks shallower than it is, by the factor of the refractive index.","steps":["Apparent depth = real depth/n.","n = 4/3 and real depth = 12 cm.","12 ÷ (4/3) = 12 × 3/4 = 9 cm."],"others":"12 cm ignores refraction. 16 cm multiplies instead of dividing. 8 cm uses the wrong ratio.","trap":"Apparent depth is smaller than real depth when you look from the rarer medium.","answer":"9 cm"},"phy-11-08-m11":{"idea":"Total internal reflection needs light going from a denser to a rarer medium at a large enough angle.","steps":["Going into a rarer medium, light bends away from the normal.","Beyond the critical angle the refracted angle would pass 90°.","Then no light leaves, and all of it reflects back into the denser medium."],"others":"From a rarer to a denser medium it cannot happen at any angle. At an angle below the critical angle some light refracts out.","trap":"Two conditions: denser to rarer, and angle bigger than the critical angle.","answer":"from a denser to a rarer medium, at an angle of incidence greater than the critical angle"},"phy-11-08-m12":{"idea":"At the critical angle the refracted ray just grazes the boundary, at 90°.","steps":["Put r = 90° in Snell's law: n sin i_c = 1 × sin 90°.","sin 90° = 1, so n sin i_c = 1.","Therefore sin i_c = 1/n."],"others":"cos i_c = 1/n and tan i_c = 1/n use the wrong function. sin i_c = n would be larger than 1, which is impossible.","trap":"Larger n means smaller critical angle.","answer":"sin i_c = 1/n"},"phy-11-08-m13":{"idea":"Find the effect that is not mainly total internal reflection.","steps":["A rainbow comes from refraction, dispersion and an ordinary reflection inside each drop.","The angle inside the drop is less than the critical angle, so it is only partial reflection.","Optical fibres, diamonds and prism binoculars depend on total internal reflection.","So the rainbow is the odd one out."],"others":"Optical fibres use repeated total internal reflection. A diamond sparkles because its critical angle is small. Right-angled prisms in binoculars turn light by total internal reflection.","trap":"A rainbow shows dispersion, not total internal reflection.","answer":"the formation of a rainbow by the dispersion of sunlight in the drops of water"},"phy-11-08-m14":{"idea":"The lens maker's formula gives the focal length from the refractive index and the two curvatures.","steps":["Refraction at two curved surfaces gives 1/f = (n − 1)(1/R₁ − 1/R₂).","For a double convex lens R₁ is positive and R₂ is negative.","Then 1/f is positive, so f is positive."],"others":"(n + 1) and the plus between the radii are wrong. f = (n − 1)(R₁ − R₂) is not dimensionally correct for focal length.","trap":"It is (n − 1), and the radii are subtracted as 1/R₁ − 1/R₂.","answer":"1/f = (n − 1)(1/R₁ − 1/R₂)"},"phy-11-08-m15":{"idea":"The thin lens formula looks like the mirror formula, but with a minus sign.","steps":["With the Cartesian sign convention, the lens formula is 1/f = 1/v − 1/u.","u is negative for a real object.","Magnification is m = v/u."],"others":"1/f = 1/v + 1/u is the mirror formula. f = v − u and 1/f = u − v are not valid.","trap":"Lens: minus. Mirror: plus.","answer":"1/f = 1/v − 1/u, with the Cartesian sign convention"},"phy-11-08-m16":{"idea":"A convex lens with the object at 2f forms a real image at 2f on the other side, same size.","steps":["Take u = −2f in 1/v = 1/f + 1/u.","1/v = 1/f − 1/(2f) = 1/(2f), so v = 2f.","m = v/u = −1: inverted and the same size."],"others":"Virtual and erect needs the object inside the focus. A diminished image at f is for a far object. A magnified image beyond 2f is for an object between f and 2f.","trap":"This position is used to measure f: image and object are equal in size.","answer":"real, inverted, of the same size, at a distance 2f on the other side"},"phy-11-08-m17":{"idea":"A prism bends each colour by a different amount, because the refractive index depends on wavelength.","steps":["Glass has a higher refractive index for shorter wavelengths.","Violet has the shortest wavelength, so it has the highest index.","So violet bends the most and red the least."],"others":"Red has the longest wavelength but the lowest index, so it bends the least. Green is in the middle.","trap":"Violet is bent most, red least.","answer":"violet, as its refractive index is the highest"},"phy-11-08-m18":{"idea":"At minimum deviation the ray goes symmetrically through the prism.","steps":["Then r = A/2.","And i = (A + δ_m)/2.","Snell's law n = sin i/sin r gives n = sin((A + δ_m)/2)/sin(A/2)."],"others":"The other forms drop the halves, turn the ratio upside down, or are not trigonometric ratios.","trap":"Both A and A + δ_m are halved inside the sines.","answer":"n = sin((A + δ_m)/2)/sin(A/2)"},"phy-11-08-m19":{"idea":"Air scatters blue light much more than red light, which makes the sky look blue.","steps":["Scattered intensity goes as 1/λ⁴ (Rayleigh scattering).","Blue has a shorter wavelength, so it scatters more.","The scattered blue reaches us from every part of the sky."],"others":"Red is scattered less, not more. Ultraviolet is invisible. Absorption is not the reason.","trap":"Red sunsets come from the same effect: blue is scattered away.","answer":"short wavelengths (blue) much more than long wavelengths (red)"},"phy-11-08-m20":{"idea":"Chromatic aberration is a colour fault of lenses.","steps":["Refractive index differs for each colour.","So the focal length is different for each colour.","Colours focus at different points and the image has coloured edges."],"others":"Spherical surfaces cause spherical aberration, not chromatic. Thickness at the centre and reflection at the edges are not the cause.","trap":"Spherical aberration is about shape. Chromatic aberration is about colour.","answer":"the focal length is different for the different colours of light"},"phy-11-08-m21":{"idea":"A simple microscope magnifies by 1 + D/f when the image is at the near point.","steps":["With the final image at the near point D = 25 cm, M = 1 + D/f.","With the image at infinity, M = D/f.","A shorter f gives a larger M."],"others":"D/f is the case for the image at infinity. 1 − D/f and f/D are wrong forms.","trap":"Near point: 1 + D/f. Infinity: D/f.","answer":"1 + D/f"},"phy-11-08-m22":{"idea":"A compound microscope magnifies twice: first by the objective, then by the eyepiece.","steps":["The objective forms a real, magnified image.","The eyepiece magnifies that image again.","The total is the product M = m_o × M_e."],"others":"A ratio of focal lengths is the telescope rule. Adding or subtracting magnifications does not give the combined effect.","trap":"Magnifications multiply.","answer":"the product of the magnification of the objective and that of the eyepiece"},"phy-11-08-m23":{"idea":"A telescope in normal adjustment magnifies by the ratio of the two focal lengths.","steps":["The objective has a long focal length f_o and the eyepiece a short one f_e.","In normal adjustment the final image is at infinity.","M = f_o/f_e."],"others":"f_e/f_o would be less than 1. The sum f_o + f_e is the length of the telescope. The product has no meaning here.","trap":"Objective on top, eyepiece below.","answer":"f_o/f_e, the ratio of the focal lengths of the objective and the eyepiece"},"phy-11-08-n01":{"idea":"Two mirrors at an angle θ give 360°/θ − 1 images.","steps":["θ = 60°, so 360/60 = 6.","Number of images = 6 − 1 = 5."],"trap":"The formula works when 360/θ is a whole number.","answer":"5"},"phy-11-08-n02":{"idea":"Use the mirror formula 1/v + 1/u = 1/f with real distances taken as positive.","steps":["f = 10 cm and u = 30 cm.","1/v = 1/10 − 1/30 = 3/30 − 1/30 = 2/30.","v = 30/2 = 15.0 cm."],"trap":"The image is real, inverted and smaller, as u > 2f.","answer":"15.0 cm"},"phy-11-08-n03":{"idea":"A ray bends at the surface so that Snell's law holds at the point where it crosses.","steps":["Let x be the horizontal distance from the point below A to the crossing point.","Then sin i = x/√(x² + 16) and sin r = (5 − x)/√((5 − x)² + 36).","Snell's law: sin i = 1.5 sin r.","Solving this equation gives x = 2.63 cm."],"trap":"Light takes the path of least time; the same x makes the total time smallest.","answer":"2.63 cm"},"phy-11-08-n04":{"idea":"The critical angle comes from sin i_c = 1/n.","steps":["sin i_c = 1/1.5 = 0.6667.","i_c = sin⁻¹(0.6667).","i_c = 41.81°."],"trap":"For water (n = 1.33) it is about 48.6°. Higher n gives a smaller angle.","answer":"41.81°"},"phy-11-08-n05":{"idea":"Apparent depth is real depth divided by the refractive index.","steps":["Real depth = 12 cm and n = 4/3.","Apparent depth = 12/(4/3).","= 12 × 3/4 = 9.00 cm."],"trap":"Always less than the real depth when viewed from above.","answer":"9.00 cm"},"phy-11-08-n06":{"idea":"Use the lens formula in the form 1/v − 1/u = 1/f with the Cartesian signs.","steps":["u = −30 cm and f = +20 cm.","1/v = 1/f + 1/u = 1/20 − 1/30 = 3/60 − 2/60 = 1/60.","v = 60.0 cm (real image on the other side)."],"trap":"The image is twice as large because m = v/u = 60/(−30) = −2.","answer":"60.0 cm"},"phy-11-08-n07":{"idea":"Use the lens maker's formula with the signs of the radii.","steps":["1/f = (n − 1)(1/R₁ − 1/R₂).","n − 1 = 0.5, 1/R₁ = 1/15 and 1/R₂ = −1/30.","1/f = 0.5 × (1/15 + 1/30) = 0.5 × 0.1 = 0.05.","f = 1/0.05 = 20.0 cm."],"trap":"R₂ is negative, so subtracting it adds.","answer":"20.0 cm"},"phy-11-08-n08":{"idea":"At minimum deviation the angle of refraction is A/2.","steps":["r = A/2 = 30°.","sin i = n sin r = 1.5 × 0.5 = 0.75, so i = 48.59°.","δ_m = 2i − A = 97.18° − 60°.","δ_m = 37.18°."],"trap":"At minimum deviation i = (A + δ_m)/2, so δ_m = 2i − A.","answer":"37.18°"},"phy-11-08-n09":{"idea":"A simple microscope with the image at the near point has M = 1 + D/f.","steps":["D = 25 cm and f = 5.0 cm.","D/f = 5.","M = 1 + 5 = 6.0."],"trap":"If the image were at infinity, M would be 5.0 instead.","answer":"6.0"},"phy-11-08-n10":{"idea":"The compound microscope's total magnification is the product of the objective's and the eyepiece's.","steps":["Objective: 1/v = 1/1.0 − 1/1.05, so v = 21 cm and m_o = v/u = 21/1.05 = 20.","Eyepiece with the image at 25 cm: M_e = 1 + D/f_e = 1 + 25/5.0 = 6.","Total M = 20 × 6 = 120."],"trap":"Multiply the two magnifications; do not add them.","answer":"120"}};
